\title{Nonexistence of Polynomial Solutions for Airy's Equation and Its Riccati Form}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We prove two sharp nonexistence results concerning Airy's differential
equation, the classical second-order linear equation $y'' = x\,y$ that governs
wave amplitudes near a caustic. Working purely algebraically in the polynomial
ring $\R[x]$ equipped with the formal derivative, we show that the only
polynomial $p$ satisfying $p'' = x\,p$ is the zero polynomial, and that the
associated Riccati equation $u' + u^2 = x$, obtained from the logarithmic
derivative substitution, has no polynomial solution at all. Both proofs are
elementary degree-counting arguments, requiring no analysis, no convergence
considerations, and no real-valued functions: they rest only on the additivity
of degree under multiplication, the strict degree drop under differentiation,
and the dominance of a strictly higher-degree summand. We isolate the structural
core of these arguments, explain why they generalize to equations of the form
$p'' = q\,p$ for any coefficient $q$ of positive degree and to higher-order
analogues $p^{(k)} = x\,p$, and discuss the consequences for the transcendence
of the Airy function and for the differential algebra of equations with
polynomial coefficients.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Airy's equation
\begin{equation}
  y'' = x\,y \label{eq:airy}
\end{equation}
arose in George Biddell Airy's 1838 study of the intensity of light near a
caustic. It is the simplest second-order linear ordinary differential equation
whose coefficient varies linearly with the independent variable, and as such it
sits at a boundary: for $x < 0$ its solutions oscillate, while for $x > 0$ they
behave exponentially. Its solutions, the Airy functions $\Ai(x)$ and $\Bi(x)$,
are ubiquitous in optics, semiclassical quantum mechanics, the asymptotics of
orthogonal polynomials, and the Tracy--Widom laws of random matrix theory.

Despite the analytic richness of its solutions, equation~\eqref{eq:airy} has a
coefficient, $x$, that is itself a polynomial. It is therefore natural to ask
whether \eqref{eq:airy} admits solutions within the simplest possible function
class --- the polynomials. Many classical equations do: Legendre's, Hermite's,
Laguerre's, and Chebyshev's equations all possess polynomial eigensolutions. The
purpose of this paper is to establish, with complete rigor and by entirely
elementary means, that Airy's equation does \emph{not}.

We prove two results.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{(Linear form.)} The only $p \in \R[x]$ with $p'' = x\,p$ is
    $p = 0$.
  \item \textbf{(Riccati form.)} No $p \in \R[x]$ satisfies $p'' + (p')^2 = x$.
\end{enumerate}
The Riccati form is the equation satisfied by an antiderivative of a logarithmic
derivative of a solution of \eqref{eq:airy}; we elaborate on this connection in
Section~\ref{sec:riccati-link}. Both proofs are degree-counting arguments carried
out in the formal polynomial ring, so they make no reference to limits,
convergence, or real-analytic structure.

\section{Preliminaries: the degree calculus}\label{sec:prelim}

Throughout, $\R[x]$ denotes the ring of polynomials in one indeterminate $x$ over
the real numbers, and for a polynomial $p$ we write $p'$ for its \emph{formal
derivative}, defined termwise by $\frac{d}{dx} x^n = n\,x^{n-1}$ and extended
linearly. For a nonzero polynomial $p$, $\deg p$ denotes its \emph{degree}. We
record the facts that drive every argument.

\begin{lemma}[Degree of a product with $x$]\label{lem:mulx}
For any nonzero $p \in \R[x]$, $\deg(x\,p) = \deg p + 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Multiplication by $x$ sends $\sum_k a_k x^k$ to $\sum_k a_k x^{k+1}$. If $a_n$ is
the leading coefficient of $p$ (so $a_n \ne 0$ and $n = \deg p$), then
$a_n x^{n+1}$ is the leading term of $x\,p$, nonzero because $\R$ has no zero
divisors. Hence $\deg(x\,p) = n + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree drop under differentiation]\label{lem:deriv}
For any $p \in \R[x]$, $\deg(p') \le \deg p - 1$, and consequently
$\deg(p'') \le \deg p - 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Differentiating $\sum_k a_k x^k$ gives $\sum_k k\,a_k x^{k-1}$, every term of
which has degree strictly less than the corresponding term of $p$. Applying the
bound twice gives $\deg(p'') \le \deg p - 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Dominance of a strictly larger summand]\label{lem:dom}
If $a, b \in \R[x]$ satisfy $\deg a < \deg b$, then $\deg(a + b) = \deg b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient of $x^{\deg b}$ in $a + b$ equals the leading coefficient of $b$
(since $a$ contributes nothing in that degree), which is nonzero, and no
higher-degree term appears.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree of a square]\label{lem:sq}
For any $p \in \R[x]$, $\deg(p^2) = 2 \deg p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The leading term of $p^2$ is the square of the leading term of $p$, whose
coefficient is the square of a nonzero real and hence nonzero.
\end{proof}

These four lemmas are the entire toolbox.

\section{The linear form}

\begin{theorem}[No nonzero polynomial solution of Airy's equation]\label{thm:lin}
Let $p \in \R[x]$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
  p'' = x\,p. \label{eq:lin}
\end{equation}
Then $p = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose for contradiction that $p \ne 0$, and set $n = \deg p$. Comparing the
two sides of \eqref{eq:lin} as equal polynomials, they must have equal degree. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:mulx}, $\deg(x\,p) = n + 1$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:deriv},
$\deg(p'') \le n - 2$. Since $p'' = x\,p$,
\[
  n + 1 = \deg(p'') \le n - 2,
\]
whence $n + 1 \le n - 2$, i.e. $3 \le 0$, a contradiction. Therefore $p = 0$.
Conversely $p = 0$ does satisfy \eqref{eq:lin}, since $0 = x \cdot 0$.
\end{proof}

The proof exposes the mechanism precisely: multiplication by $x$ raises the
degree by one, while two differentiations lower it by two, creating a permanent
gap of three between the two sides.

\section{The Riccati form}

We now turn to the nonlinear companion equation
\begin{equation}
  p'' + (p')^2 = x. \label{eq:ric}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[No polynomial solution of the Riccati form]\label{thm:ric}
There is no $p \in \R[x]$ satisfying $p'' + (p')^2 = x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose such a $p$ exists. Write $q = p'$ and let $d = \deg q$ (with $d = 0$ when
$q$ is constant). By Lemma~\ref{lem:sq}, $\deg(q^2) = 2d$, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:deriv} applied to $q = p'$, $\deg(p'') = \deg(q') \le d - 1$.

\emph{Case $d = 0$.} Then $q$ is constant, so $q' = p'' = 0$ and $q^2$ is
constant; thus the left-hand side $p'' + q^2$ has degree $\le 0$, while
$\deg(x) = 1$, so $1 \le 0$, a contradiction.

\emph{Case $d \ge 1$.} Then $\deg(q^2) = 2d \ge 2$ while $\deg(p'') \le d - 1 <
2d$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:dom}, $\deg\bigl(p'' + (p')^2\bigr) = \deg(q^2) = 2d$.
Equation~\eqref{eq:ric} forces $2d = \deg(x) = 1$ with $d \ge 1$, a contradiction
(indeed $2d \ge 2 > 1$).

In both cases we reach a contradiction, so no polynomial $p$ satisfies
\eqref{eq:ric}.
\end{proof}

The asymmetry between Theorems~\ref{thm:lin} and~\ref{thm:ric} is worth
emphasizing: the linear equation is homogeneous, so $p = 0$ is automatically a
solution and the theorem is a uniqueness statement; the Riccati equation is
inhomogeneous, so even the zero polynomial fails and the conclusion is outright
nonexistence.

\section{From the linear equation to the Riccati equation}\label{sec:riccati-link}

The two theorems are not independent curiosities; the Riccati equation is the
standard nonlinear shadow of the linear one. Given a (sufficiently smooth,
locally nonvanishing) solution $y$ of $y'' = x\,y$, introduce the logarithmic
derivative $w = y'/y$. Then
\[
  w' = \frac{y''}{y} - \left(\frac{y'}{y}\right)^2 = x - w^2,
  \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad w' + w^2 = x.
\]
Writing $w = p'$ for an antiderivative $p$ recasts this as \eqref{eq:ric}. Thus a
polynomial solution of the Riccati form would correspond to a solution of Airy's
equation whose logarithmic derivative is a polynomial; Theorem~\ref{thm:ric}
shows no such closed form exists. The analytic substitution is given only to
motivate the algebraic statement, whose proof never leaves the polynomial ring.

\section{Generalizations}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial coefficient of positive degree]\label{thm:gen}
Let $q \in \R[x]$ be a fixed nonzero polynomial with $\deg q \ge 1$. Then the
only $p \in \R[x]$ satisfying $p'' = q\,p$ is $p = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
If $p \ne 0$ with $\deg p = n$, then $\deg(q\,p) = \deg q + n \ge n + 1$ by
additivity of degree, while $\deg(p'') \le n - 2$. The inequality $n + 1 \le
\deg(p'') \le n - 2$ is impossible. Airy's equation is the case $q = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Higher-order analogue]\label{thm:high}
For any integer $k \ge 1$, the only $p \in \R[x]$ satisfying $p^{(k)} = x\,p$ is
$p = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
The $k$-th derivative obeys $\deg(p^{(k)}) \le n - k$, while $\deg(x\,p) = n + 1$.
Since $n + 1 > n - k$ for every $k \ge 0$, the degrees never match unless
$p = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Lemmas~\ref{lem:mulx}, \ref{lem:dom}, and~\ref{lem:sq} use only that the
coefficient ring is an integral domain, so that the leading coefficient of a
product is nonzero. Consequently Theorems~\ref{thm:lin}, \ref{thm:ric},
\ref{thm:gen}, and~\ref{thm:high} hold verbatim over any integral domain $R$ in
place of $\R$, e.g.\ $\Z$, $\mathbb{Q}$, or $\mathbb{C}$. The real numbers play
no special role.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion and consequences}

\textbf{Transcendence of the Airy function.} Theorem~\ref{thm:lin} is the first
rung on the ladder of statements asserting that the Airy function is not
``elementary.'' Because the homogeneous solution space of \eqref{eq:airy} is
two-dimensional and contains no nonzero polynomial, every nonzero solution must
be genuinely transcendental over the polynomials. The Airy function does possess
an everywhere-convergent power series and the integral representation
$\Ai(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^\infty \cos\!\left(\frac{t^3}{3} + xt\right)\,dt$,
but it cannot be captured by any finite polynomial expression.

\textbf{The value of negative results.} Nonexistence theorems function as
signposts: a symbolic ODE solver searching for polynomial solutions of
\eqref{eq:airy} can terminate immediately with the certificate provided by
Theorem~\ref{thm:lin}, and a search for a polynomial logarithmic derivative can
be pruned by Theorem~\ref{thm:ric}.

\textbf{Methodological remark.} By formulating Airy's equation in the formal
polynomial ring with the formal derivative, the statement ``Airy's equation has
no polynomial solution'' becomes a finite combinatorial fact about degrees, fully
decidable by inspection of two integers.

\section{Future work}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Coefficients of arbitrary positive degree.} A fuller treatment
    would characterize exactly which polynomial coefficients $q$ admit nonzero
    polynomial solutions of $p'' = q\,p$, recovering the classical
    orthogonal-polynomial families in the boundary case $\deg q = 0$.
  \item \textbf{General base rings.} Restating all results over an arbitrary
    integral domain clarifies exactly which ring-theoretic hypotheses the degree
    calculus requires.
  \item \textbf{Higher-order and mixed analogues.} Beyond $p^{(k)} = x\,p$, one
    can study Riccati-type identities mixing several derivatives.
  \item \textbf{Formal power series.} Airy's equation does admit formal
    power-series solutions; contrasting the polynomial nonexistence proved here
    with the power-series existence theory would give a complete picture.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Airy's equation $y'' = x\,y$, for all its analytic depth, hides a purely
combinatorial secret: it cannot be solved by a polynomial, and neither can its
Riccati companion $u' + u^2 = x$. The proofs are short, elementary, and sharp ---
a comparison of two integers --- and they extend without effort to a broad family
of related equations and to any integral domain of coefficients.

